      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense but i keep seeing people call it 'spooky action at a distance'. why do they call it spooky? is there something actually weird or unsettling about the way the particles interact, or is it just a figure of speech?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The term \"spooky action at a distance\" was coined by Albert Einstein, who was initially skeptical of quantum entanglement. He thought it was too strange and unsettling, like something out of a fantasy novel. Einstein's skepticism was rooted in his classical understanding of space and time, where information can't travel faster than the speed of light.\n\nThe term \"spooky\" is indeed a figure of speech, but it's also a nod to the fact that entanglement seems to defy our intuitive understanding of space and causality. It's as if the particles are communicating with each other in a way that's faster than light, which is forbidden by the laws of classical physics. This creates a sense of unease, like something is happening behind the scenes that we can't quite grasp.\n\nIn a way, the term \"spooky\" is a reflection of the fact that entanglement is a fundamentally non-intuitive phenomenon. It's not just a matter of saying \"oh, it's weird,\" but rather that it challenges our fundamental understanding of the world. And that's what makes it so fascinating – it's a reminder that there's still so much we don't understand about the universe, and that's what makes it so \"sp",
